window.LESSONS["d9-iabp"] = {
  id: "d9-iabp",
  day: 10,
  ord: 1,
  title: "IABP & Temporary Support",
  short: "IABP",
  minutes: 16,
  chunks: [
    {
      title: "Rationale for Mechanical Circulatory Support",
      html: "<p>Heart failure represents a state in which the myocardium cannot generate sufficient cardiac output to meet systemic metabolic demands. When pharmacologic support — inotropes, vasopressors, vasodilators — is insufficient to maintain perfusion of vital organs, mechanical devices can assume part or all of the cardiac pumping function.</p><p>The central goals of mechanical circulatory support (MCS) are to maintain systemic perfusion pressure, unload the failing ventricle to reduce wall stress and myocardial oxygen demand, and allow time for recovery, decision-making, or transition to definitive therapy. The degree to which a device unloads the ventricle versus simply augmenting perfusion varies considerably and is a critical distinction in device selection.</p><div class='kbox kbox-study'><div class='kbox-tag'>STUDY</div><p>The key question for every MCS device: Does it passively augment flow (like IABP) or does it actively unload the failing ventricle (like Impella)? Active unloading reduces LVEDP, wall stress, and myocardial oxygen demand — passive augmentation primarily supports the peripheral circulation.</p></div><p>Cohn frames the stakes in numbers: cardiogenic shock complicates <b>2.4&ndash;12% of acute MIs</b>, and the landmark SHOCK trial showed mortality <b>above 50% even with revascularization</b> &mdash; half of these patients die despite an open artery, which is exactly why a mechanical bridge earns its place. Post-cardiotomy shock (failure to separate from bypass) is far rarer, roughly <b>0.2&ndash;0.6%</b> of cardiac operations, but is nearly always fatal if not supported effectively.</p><p>The book gives concrete triggers for escalating from drugs to a device &mdash; but only <em>after</em> TEE has excluded a surgically correctable cause (tamponade, acute papillary-muscle rupture, post-infarct VSD), because fixing those obviates the pump entirely. The hemodynamic thresholds are a <b>cardiac index &lt; 2.2 L/min/m&sup2;</b>, <b>systolic BP &lt; 90 mmHg</b>, <b>mean PCWP or CVP &gt; 20 mmHg</b>, and <b>high-dose support with at least two inotropes</b>. In the post-cardiotomy setting those same numbers <em>without</em> mechanical support carry a greater than 50% mortality. When an acute device buys time but the heart declares non-recovery, the strategy becomes a <b>&quot;bridge to a bridge&quot;</b> &mdash; a short-term pump used to reach a durable transplant-bridge VAD &mdash; though bridging older, comorbid patients directly onto a destination pump is done with caution.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "IABP Mechanism: Counterpulsation",
      html: "<p>The intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) is the most widely used short-term support device, and the whole trick is in the name: <b>counter</b>pulsation — the balloon does the exact opposite of the heart, always working in the heart's off-beat. A sausage-shaped balloon sits in the descending aorta and a console flips it in and out of phase with the cardiac cycle. It never pumps blood itself; it just shoves a column of aortic blood around at exactly the right moments to help the ventricle in two ways at once.</p><p>Picture the two benefits as perfectly timed: when the heart is <em>resting</em> (diastole) the balloon <b>inflates</b>, squeezing blood backward into the coronary arteries — a free top-up of coronary perfusion when the coronaries actually fill. Then, an instant before the heart <em>works</em> (systole), the balloon <b>deflates</b>, leaving a sudden empty space in the aorta so the ventricle ejects into a partial vacuum — less afterload, less oxygen demand. Inflate on the rest, vanish for the push. The catch is that the whole benefit depends on flawless timing.</p><ul><li><b>Diastolic inflation</b>: Balloon inflates at the onset of diastole (triggered by ECG or arterial waveform), displacing blood proximally into the aortic root — augmenting diastolic coronary perfusion pressure (diastolic augmentation)</li><li><b>Systolic deflation</b>: Balloon deflates just before systole, creating sudden reduction in aortic volume — reducing LV afterload (impedance). This decreases LV end-systolic volume, wall stress, and myocardial oxygen consumption (MVO2)</li><li><b>Net effect</b>: Modest cardiac output improvement (0.5-1.0 L/min), increased diastolic BP, decreased systolic BP, reduced MVO2</li></ul><p>Cohn puts hard numbers on the two effects. Counterpulsation cuts <b>peak systolic wall stress (afterload) by 14&ndash;19%</b> and LV systolic pressure by about <b>15%</b>; because wall stress drives myocardial oxygen consumption directly, MVO&sub2; falls in proportion. On the supply side, echo/Doppler shows <b>peak diastolic coronary flow velocity rising ~117%</b> and the coronary flow-velocity integral ~87%, and experimentally collateral flow to ischemic zones climbs up to <b>21% when mean arterial pressure exceeds 90 mmHg</b>. Note the key limitation the book stresses: the IABP <em>does not itself move or significantly redistribute blood</em> &mdash; output rises only because a better-perfused, less-loaded ventricle contracts more effectively.</p><p>Geometry and physics decide how much benefit you get. The balloon sits <b>just distal to the left subclavian artery</b>, and diastolic augmentation increases the closer it lies to the aortic valve; on inflation it should <b>nearly occlude the aorta</b>. Standard adult balloons are <b>30&ndash;40 mL</b> (the newer 50-mL balloon delivers higher augmented diastolic pressure, greater systolic unloading, and a larger drop in wedge pressure). Aortic compliance matters too: a stiff, calcified aorta augments more but risks wall injury, while a young, highly elastic aorta absorbs the displaced volume and augments less.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "IABP Timing Errors",
      html: "<p>Correct timing of IABP inflation and deflation is critical for therapeutic benefit and safety. Timing errors are visible on the arterial waveform trace and each produces a distinct and recognizable pattern. The balloon should inflate at the dicrotic notch (aortic valve closure, onset of diastole) and deflate just before the next systolic upstroke.</p><p>Timing errors are not merely subtherapeutic — some actively cause harm, particularly late deflation which inflates into the next systole and dramatically worsens afterload.</p><ul><li><b>Early inflation</b>: Balloon inflates before aortic valve closes — increases LV afterload, can cause or worsen aortic regurgitation</li><li><b>Late inflation</b>: Misses peak diastolic augmentation; reduced coronary perfusion benefit</li><li><b>Early deflation</b>: Optimal afterload reduction window is missed; suboptimal hemodynamic benefit</li><li><b>Late deflation</b>: Balloon still inflated as next systole begins — catastrophically increases afterload; the most dangerous timing error</li></ul><div class='kbox kbox-danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>DANGER</div><p>Late deflation is the most dangerous IABP timing error — the balloon remains inflated as the left ventricle contracts, creating an obstruction the ventricle must eject against. This dramatically increases afterload and myocardial oxygen demand in an already failing heart.</p></div><p>The two reference points make the errors easy to read off the trace. Inflation should coincide with <b>aortic-valve closure at the dicrotic notch</b>; deflation is practically timed to the descent of the <b>ECG R wave</b>, as late as possible to hold the augmented diastolic pressure but before the ventricle ejects. Cohn spells out why early inflation is more than just suboptimal: it <em>reduces stroke volume and raises both end-systolic and end-diastolic volumes</em>, driving afterload <em>and</em> preload up at once.</p><p>The device also demands a cooperative rhythm. Optimal timing needs a regular rate with a clean R wave or a discrete dicrotic notch; during <b>tachycardia</b> the pump is often set to inflate <b>every other beat (1:2)</b>, and during chaotic rhythms it falls back to an <b>asynchronous fixed mode</b> that may not reliably reduce afterload &mdash; which is why unstable patients are often paced into a regular rhythm so the balloon can be timed properly. Modern consoles with fiber-optic pressure transducers at the catheter tip auto-select the trigger and time inflation/deflation, reducing operator error.</p>"
    },
    {
      type: "checkpoint",
      q: "An IABP arterial waveform shows the balloon deflating just as the next systolic pressure upstroke begins. Which timing error is this, and what is its primary danger?",
      options: [
        "Early inflation — increases aortic regurgitation",
        "Early deflation — misses optimal afterload reduction window",
        "Late deflation — increases LV afterload during systole",
        "Late inflation — misses peak diastolic augmentation"
      ],
      answer: 2,
      explain: "Late deflation means the balloon is still inflated when the ventricle begins to contract for the next systole. This creates an obstruction the LV must eject against, dramatically increasing afterload and MVO2 in a failing heart — the opposite of the intended effect and the most dangerous timing error."
    },
    {
      title: "IABP Indications and Contraindications",
      html: "<p>The IABP is most effective in ischemic cardiogenic shock where coronary perfusion augmentation is a primary need. Its modest hemodynamic benefit (0.5-1.0 L/min increase in cardiac output) makes it appropriate for bridging or for moderate hemodynamic impairment, but insufficient as sole support in severe cardiogenic shock.</p><p>Contraindications stem directly from the mechanism: diastolic augmentation is the primary therapeutic action, and any condition that converts this augmentation into harm is a contraindication.</p><ul><li><b>Indications</b>: Cardiogenic shock (bridge to PCI/CABG or recovery), refractory unstable angina, post-cardiotomy low output, prophylactic pre-operative insertion in high-risk patients (poor EF, redo surgery, left main disease)</li><li><b>Contraindications</b>: Aortic regurgitation (diastolic augmentation worsens regurgitant volume), aortic dissection (balloon trauma to dissected wall), severe peripheral arterial disease (access and limb ischemia risk), severe aortic aneurysm</li></ul><div class='kbox kbox-pearl'><div class='kbox-tag'>PEARL</div><p>The IABP-SHOCK II trial (2012) found no significant 30-day mortality reduction with IABP plus early revascularization vs. revascularization alone in AMI-complicated cardiogenic shock. This landmark RCT has led to downgraded guideline recommendations for routine IABP use in AMI-CS, though it remains in use for specific cardiac surgery indications.</p></div><p>Cohn broadens the indication list well past the classic triad (cardiogenic shock, uncontrolled ischemic pain, post-cardiotomy low output). Add <b>high-grade left-main stenosis</b>, <b>high-risk or failed PCI/atherectomy/stenting</b>, poorly controlled peri-operative ventricular arrhythmias, and the mechanical MI complications &mdash; <b>post-infarction VSD and acute mitral regurgitation</b> &mdash; where afterload reduction shrinks the shunt or regurgitant fraction while surgery is arranged. As a bridge, an IABP stabilizes over half of patients awaiting transplant or LVAD, and a version placed through a graft sewn to the <b>subclavian artery</b> even allows the patient to <b>ambulate</b> (reported 93% success) and qualifies them as UNOS status 1A.</p><p>Know the outcome numbers behind the caution. IABP-SHOCK II randomized 600 AMI-shock patients and found essentially <b>no 30-day mortality difference (39.7% vs 41.3%)</b>. Complications run <b>~12.9&ndash;29% (average ~20%)</b>, dominated by <b>leg ischemia (9&ndash;25%, transient signs in up to 47%)</b>; balloon rupture occurs in ~1.7%. Overall hospital mortality is high (<b>26&ndash;50%</b>) &mdash; driven by the shock that prompted the device, not the device itself. Each contraindication maps to the mechanism: severe AR turns diastolic augmentation into extra regurgitant volume, and aortic dissection or aneurysm risks catastrophic wall injury from the pulsating balloon.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "The Impella Device Family",
      html: "<p>Where the IABP only nudges the circulation from the sidelines, the Impella actually does the pumping. It is a tiny motorized <b>Archimedes screw on the end of a catheter</b> — an impeller sitting across the aortic valve with its intake in the left ventricle and its outlet in the aorta, spinning fast enough to suck blood straight out of the failing LV and fling it into the circulation. Because it physically empties the ventricle, it does what the IABP cannot: <b>true unloading</b>, dropping LV preload and LVEDP directly rather than just tinkering with afterload from outside.</p><p>This active transvalvular pumping produces direct LV preload reduction and LVEDP reduction — true ventricular unloading rather than the passive counterpulsation of the IABP. The degree of support scales with device size, from the catheter-based Impella 2.5 through the surgically-placed Impella 5.5.</p><ul><li><b>Impella 2.5</b>: approximately 2.5 L/min; percutaneous insertion</li><li><b>Impella CP</b>: approximately 4 L/min; percutaneous insertion; most commonly used</li><li><b>Impella 5.0 / 5.5</b>: approximately 5 L/min; requires surgical cut-down for insertion</li><li><b>Positioning</b>: inlet port 3.5 cm below aortic valve annulus into LV cavity; confirmed by TTE/TEE or fluoroscopy</li></ul><p>Cohn classifies the Impella as a <b>microaxial pump</b> &mdash; the rotor spins parallel to the blood path, unlike the perpendicular-flow centrifugal pumps used for ECMO. Two design details matter clinically. First, the narrow cannula lets the aortic leaflets <b>coapt around it</b>, so it produces minimal aortic insufficiency despite crossing the valve. Second, pressure transducers on <em>each</em> end straddle the aortic valve, so the console can detect displacement and let you reposition without fresh imaging. The dual mechanism is the whole point: active forward flow raises net cardiac output while simultaneously <b>unloading the ventricle (lowering MVO&sub2;)</b> and augmenting coronary flow &mdash; supply up, demand down.</p><p>Match device to access and flow need. The percutaneous <b>2.5 (12F, up to 2.5 L/min)</b> is now largely reserved for protected high-risk PCI; the <b>CP (14F, ~4 L/min)</b> is the workhorse for rapid stabilization in low-output states; the <b>5.0/5.5 (21F)</b> gives full ~5 L/min support but needs a surgical cut-down or an <b>axillary-artery graft</b>. Crucially, like every left-sided pump the delivered flow depends on adequate LV filling &mdash; so it is limited by <b>RV function, pulmonary vascular resistance, and volume status</b>. For isolated right-heart failure the <b>Impella RP</b> (22F via femoral vein, across the pulmonary valve, IVC&rarr;PA, &gt;4 L/min) is the mirror-image device.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "Impella Management Priorities",
      html: "<p>Correct positioning is critical and must be maintained continuously — device migration is common with changes in patient position or hemodynamics. Too deep places the inlet against the LV apex or mitral subvalvular apparatus causing hemolysis, arrhythmia, and impaired function. Too shallow places the device above the aortic valve, causing it to pump aortic blood rather than unload the LV.</p><p>A suction event is a critical alarm indicating the Impella is pumping faster than the LV is filling — the inlet ports draw LV walls against the inlet. This is the opposite problem from what the device should be doing and worsens hemolysis and arrhythmia.</p><ul><li><b>Monitor placement</b>: auscultation of position signal, echo, fluoroscopy — reposition frequently</li><li><b>Monitor hemolysis</b>: plasma-free hemoglobin, LDH, haptoglobin, urinalysis for hemoglobinuria</li><li><b>Anticoagulation</b>: heparin infusion targeting ACT 160-180 seconds</li><li><b>Suction event response</b>: reduce pump speed, give volume if hypovolemic, confirm position, assess for RV failure, rule out tamponade — never increase pump speed against a suction event</li></ul><div class='kbox kbox-danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>DANGER</div><p>Impella is contraindicated in moderate-to-severe aortic regurgitation (device crosses the aortic valve and worsens AR), mechanical aortic valve, LV thrombus, and severe peripheral arterial disease.</p></div><p>Think of the suction event through Cohn's flow physiology: because Impella output is <b>preload-dependent</b> (it can only pump what the LV delivers), an alarm for suction usually means the ventricle is <em>under-filled</em>, not that the LV has recovered. The two commonest culprits are therefore <b>hypovolemia</b> and <b>right-heart failure reducing LV preload</b> &mdash; which is why the reflex to crank up speed is exactly wrong: a faster impeller against an empty ventricle only pulls myocardium and subvalvular tissue into the inlet, worsening hemolysis and arrhythmia. Confirm position too, since a migrated inlet mimics the same picture.</p><p>The device runs a continuous <b>purge circuit</b> &mdash; a heparinized dextrose solution pushed through the motor to keep blood from entering and clotting the rotor bearings &mdash; on top of systemic heparin targeting an ACT of roughly 160&ndash;180 s. Hemolysis surveillance is central to safe use: trend <b>plasma-free hemoglobin, LDH, and haptoglobin</b> and watch the urine for hemoglobinuria, because a malpositioned or suctioning pump shears red cells. And the same reasoning behind avoiding it in moderate-or-greater AR (it sits across the valve and worsens the leak) explains the mechanical-valve and LV-thrombus contraindications &mdash; the catheter cannot safely traverse a mechanical leaflet, and it would dislodge apical clot.</p>"
    },
    {
      type: "checkpoint",
      q: "An Impella CP device alarms for a suction event. The patient is tachycardic and mildly hypotensive. What is the correct immediate response?",
      options: [
        "Increase pump speed to overcome the obstruction",
        "Initiate ECMO immediately",
        "Remove the device and place an IABP",
        "Reduce speed and restore LV filling"
      ],
      answer: 3,
      explain: "A suction event means the Impella is pumping faster than the LV is filling — increasing pump speed worsens the event. The correct response is: reduce speed to allow the LV to fill, assess and treat hypovolemia, confirm device position (may have migrated), assess for RV failure reducing LV preload, and rule out tamponade."
    }
  ]
};

window.LESSONS["d9-ecmo"] = {
  id: "d9-ecmo",
  day: 10,
  ord: 2,
  title: "ECMO: VA & VV",
  short: "ECMO",
  minutes: 16,
  chunks: [
    {
      title: "VA-ECMO: Mechanism and Hemodynamics",
      html: "<p>VA-ECMO is essentially a <b>portable heart-lung machine for the ICU</b> — the same idea as the bypass circuit from the operating room, but run for days. It drains venous blood, passes it through an artificial lung to add oxygen and remove CO₂, and pumps it back into an <em>artery</em>, taking over for both a failed heart and failed lungs at once. At 3–6 L/min it can carry nearly the whole circulation, far more than an IABP or Impella.</p><p>But it hides a cruel paradox, and this is the single most important thing to understand about it: the blood returned through a femoral artery flows <b>backward up the aorta</b>, straight against the direction the left ventricle is trying to eject. So while ECMO rescues the body's perfusion, it hands the already-failing LV a stiff <b>headwind</b> — its afterload goes <em>up</em>. If the ventricle is weak enough, it can no longer push the door open against that flow: the LV distends, LVEDP climbs, pulmonary edema worsens, and the aortic valve may stop opening altogether. Supporting the body can throttle the heart — which is why VA-ECMO is so often paired with a venting device (Impella, IABP, or a surgical vent) to unload the LV.</p><ul><li><b>Benefits</b>: Augments systemic perfusion (3-6 L/min), oxygenates blood, supports both cardiac and respiratory failure simultaneously</li><li><b>Key liability</b>: Retrograde arterial return from the femoral artery increases LV afterload — opposes LV ejection</li><li><b>Consequence in severe LV failure</b>: LV may dilate, LVEDP rises, pulmonary edema worsens, aortic valve may stop opening entirely</li></ul><div class='kbox kbox-danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>DANGER</div><p>VA-ECMO supports the body but may worsen the heart. A clue to LV distension on ECMO: aortic valve stops opening on echo, pulmonary edema develops or worsens, PCWP rises despite adequate systemic perfusion. Active LV unloading (Impella, surgical LV vent, or IABP) may be required alongside ECMO.</p></div><p>Cohn treats ECLS as CPB's principles stretched over days: same circuit logic, but with <b>lower heparin doses</b>, no reversal, and none of the stasis-prone cardiotomy suction or venous reservoir. The three core components are a <b>hollow-fiber membrane oxygenator with an integrated heat exchanger</b> and a <b>centrifugal pump</b> &mdash; where <b>sweep-gas flow controls CO&sub2; clearance</b> and the <b>FiO&sub2; controls oxygenation</b> independently. Typical flows are <b>4&ndash;6 L/min at 3000&ndash;3200 rpm</b>; higher speeds are deliberately avoided because they shear blood cells. The centrifugal pump is nonocclusive and afterload-dependent, so significant hypertension <em>reduces</em> forward flow (a flowmeter is mandatory), but it also cannot rupture the arterial line or cavitate.</p><p>The book makes the afterload paradox precise. ECLS <b>unloads the right ventricle but not a compromised left</b>: in a normal heart the reduced preload plus small afterload rise shrinks end-diastolic volume, but in a dilated, poorly contracting LV the added afterload <em>offsets</em> any preload benefit &mdash; the ventricle cannot eject against it, so it stays distended and wall stress and MVO&sub2; actually climb. That stagnant, non-ejecting LV is a thrombus trap: in one series <b>20% developed LV thrombus on TEE</b>, and giving <b>protamine after starting ECLS can precipitate intracardiac clot</b>. Hence the venting imperative when the wedge stays high.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "North-South Syndrome (Harlequin Syndrome)",
      html: "<p>North-South Syndrome (also called Harlequin Syndrome or differential hypoxia) is a life-threatening complication unique to peripheral femoral VA-ECMO. It occurs when the native heart has sufficient residual function to eject blood antegradely into the ascending aorta while the lungs are severely injured — creating a situation where the brain and coronary arteries receive poorly oxygenated native cardiac output while the ECMO circuit delivers well-oxygenated blood only to the lower body.</p><p>The \"meeting point\" of native anterograde flow and ECMO retrograde flow in the descending aorta determines which territories receive oxygenated versus deoxygenated blood. If the lungs are injured, the upper body (brain, heart) may be profoundly hypoxic despite acceptable lower body oxygenation and normal hemodynamics.</p><ul><li><b>Clinical sign</b>: SpO2 from right hand (upper body, native circulation) lower than SpO2 from foot probe (lower body, ECMO circulation)</li><li><b>Treatment options</b>:</li><li>Optimize ventilation and PEEP to improve native lung oxygenation</li><li>Increase ECMO flows</li><li>Add venous drainage cannula at SVC (VVA configuration)</li><li>Transition to central ECMO cannulation (aortic return instead of femoral)</li></ul><div class='kbox kbox-mnemonic'><div class='kbox-tag'>MNEMONIC</div><p><b>North-South</b>: North (upper body/brain) gets hypoxic native blood; South (lower body) gets oxygenated ECMO blood. Check SpO2 on the right hand AND the foot when any peripheral VA-ECMO patient deteriorates.</p></div><p>The mechanism hinges on a <b>mixing point</b> (watershed) somewhere in the aorta where native antegrade output collides with the ECMO circuit's retrograde flow. As the heart recovers, that meeting point migrates <em>distally</em>, and the more the native heart contributes, the more of the arch &mdash; coronaries, then right arm, then head &mdash; is fed by blood that just passed through injured, poorly oxygenating lungs. Sample the <b>right radial artery</b> specifically, because the innominate (right subclavian/carotid) origin is the most proximal great vessel and therefore the first territory to turn hypoxic; a foot probe on ECMO blood looks reassuringly pink while the brain is starving.</p><p>Cohn's escalation ladder for differential hypoxia works from least to most invasive: first <b>optimize the native lung</b> (recruit with PEEP and ventilator settings, treat the primary injury) so the heart's own output carries more oxygen; then <b>raise ECMO flow</b> to push the mixing point back toward the arch; then convert to a hybrid <b>veno-arterial-venous configuration</b> by adding an oxygenated return limb into the SVC/internal jugular so the upper body is supplied from the venous side; and finally move to <b>central or right-axillary cannulation</b>, returning oxygenated blood into the ascending aorta itself, which eliminates the watershed entirely.</p>"
    },
    {
      type: "checkpoint",
      q: "A patient on peripheral femoral VA-ECMO has SpO2 91% on the right hand and SpO2 99% on the right foot. What is the diagnosis and mechanism?",
      options: [
        "North-South syndrome from hypoxic native upper-body flow",
        "ECMO circuit failure — the oxygenator is not functioning",
        "The foot probe is malfunctioning — single-site SpO2 is more reliable",
        "Right subclavian artery stenosis causing reduced arm perfusion"
      ],
      answer: 0,
      explain: "SpO2 higher in the foot than the hand on peripheral VA-ECMO is the signature of North-South (Harlequin) Syndrome. The native heart is ejecting blood from the injured lungs (poorly oxygenated) into the ascending aorta supplying the upper body. The ECMO circuit returns oxygenated blood retrogradely from the femoral artery, supplying the lower body. The brain and coronary arteries are receiving hypoxic blood."
    },
    {
      title: "VA-ECMO Indications and Complications",
      html: "<p>VA-ECMO is reserved for the most severe forms of cardiogenic or cardiorespiratory failure — situations where no other device can provide sufficient support. Its ability to support both cardiac and pulmonary function simultaneously makes it unique among MCS devices but also introduces a broader spectrum of complications.</p><p>Limb ischemia deserves special attention: the arterial return cannula in the femoral artery can critically reduce distal limb perfusion. A distal perfusion cannula placed in the superficial femoral artery (SFA) is standard practice to maintain limb viability and must be confirmed to be functioning throughout the ECMO run.</p><ul><li><b>Indications</b>: Refractory cardiogenic shock, cardiac arrest (ECPR — ECMO-assisted CPR), post-cardiotomy shock (failure to wean from CPB), massive PE with hemodynamic collapse, severe myocarditis, biventricular failure</li><li><b>Complications</b>: Limb ischemia (distal perfusion cannula is mandatory), bleeding (circuit anticoagulation), thromboembolism, hemolysis, circuit oxygenator failure, infection, HIT (heparin-induced thrombocytopenia)</li></ul><p>Cohn highlights the cannulation flexibility that makes ECLS the emergency device: in post-cardiotomy failure the <b>existing right-atrial and aortic cannulas</b> can be used directly, or the system converted to <b>peripheral cannulas</b> so the patient can later be decannulated <em>without reopening the chest</em>. In arrest or catheterization-lab collapse, peripheral femoral access restores circulation without a trip to the OR. Central or axillary return is chosen when peripheral disease blocks access or when highly oxygenated blood must reach the coronaries and brain directly.</p><p>The complication burden is heavy and worth memorizing from the Cleveland Clinic series: <b>re-exploration ~52%</b>, <b>dialysis-requiring renal failure ~47%</b>, <b>oxygenator change ~43%</b>, <b>bacteremia/mediastinitis ~23%</b>, <b>stroke ~10%</b>, and <b>leg ischemia up to 70%</b> (some needing thrombectomy or amputation). A <b>distal perfusion cannula (8&ndash;10F) in the superficial femoral artery</b> is standard to keep the leg alive, but remember that limb loss also comes from <em>venous</em> obstruction by the large drainage cannula &mdash; sometimes a distal venous drain is needed too. Survival tracks the underlying disaster: roughly <b>76% at 3 days, 38% at 30 days, 24% at 5 years</b>, though 30-day survivors have a 63% chance of being alive at 5 years &mdash; the early period is the killer.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "VV-ECMO vs. VA-ECMO",
      html: "<p>Venovenous ECMO (VV-ECMO) provides respiratory support only — it does not contribute to cardiac output. Blood is drained from the venous system, oxygenated by the circuit membrane lung, and returned to the venous system (typically femoral vein to jugular vein, or dual-lumen bicaval cannula via the internal jugular vein). The oxygenated blood passes through the native heart and lungs before reaching the systemic circulation, so the device does not bypass the heart.</p><p>The fundamental distinction between VA and VV ECMO lies in the arterial return: VA-ECMO returns blood to the arterial side (providing hemodynamic support), VV-ECMO returns to the venous side (providing only respiratory support while relying on native cardiac function).</p><ul><li><b>VV-ECMO</b>: Respiratory failure only (ARDS, severe pneumonia); requires adequate native cardiac function; no hemodynamic support; no LV afterload increase</li><li><b>VA-ECMO</b>: Cardiac or combined cardiorespiratory failure; provides hemodynamic support; increases LV afterload; North-South syndrome risk</li><li><b>Conversion</b>: A patient on VV-ECMO who develops cardiogenic shock may need conversion to VA-ECMO</li></ul><div class='kbox kbox-study'><div class='kbox-tag'>STUDY</div><p>Shortcut: VV = lungs only (Ventilatory); VA = heart + lungs (ventricular + arterial). If the patient's heart is failing, VV-ECMO is insufficient — they need VA-ECMO or an additional cardiac MCS device.</p></div><p>The two VV cannulation strategies each have a signature trade-off. A <b>femoral-drainage / internal-jugular-return</b> setup is quick but prone to <b>recirculation</b> &mdash; freshly oxygenated blood being re-aspirated by a nearby drainage port instead of reaching the body, the efficiency problem unique to VV. The <b>dual-lumen bicaval cannula</b> placed through the IJ solves much of this by draining from the SVC/IVC and jetting oxygenated blood across the tricuspid, and it frees the groin for mobility. Because VV support carries no arterial cannula, Cohn notes it is typically placed <b>fully percutaneously</b> &mdash; less bleeding, no surgical exposure &mdash; whereas VA arterial cannulation is usually done under direct vision to avoid iatrogenic injury and malposition.</p><p>The conversion logic is a common exam pivot. A VV patient whose ARDS drives severe pulmonary hypertension and <b>acute RV failure</b>, or who develops cardiogenic shock, has outgrown respiratory-only support: they need conversion to <b>VA-ECMO</b> or the addition of a right-sided device (e.g., a dual-lumen RV-support cannula). The reverse mistake &mdash; reaching for VA in isolated respiratory failure with a good heart &mdash; needlessly loads the LV and invites North-South syndrome, which is why the preserved-EF ARDS patient belongs on VV.</p>"
    },
    {
      type: "checkpoint",
      q: "A 28-year-old with severe ARDS from influenza has refractory hypoxemia on maximal ventilator settings. Echocardiography shows LVEF 55% with normal right ventricular function. Which ECMO configuration is most appropriate?",
      options: [
        "VV-ECMO for isolated respiratory failure",
        "VA-ECMO via femoral cannulation",
        "VA-ECMO plus Impella for LV unloading",
        "IABP for coronary perfusion augmentation"
      ],
      answer: 0,
      explain: "With preserved biventricular function and isolated respiratory failure, VV-ECMO provides oxygenation support without the hemodynamic complexity of VA-ECMO (no LV afterload increase, no North-South syndrome risk). VA-ECMO is indicated when cardiac function is impaired. IABP provides no respiratory support and has no role in isolated ARDS."
    },
    {
      title: "Managing the ECMO-LV Interaction",
      html: "<p>The most important physiologic problem specific to VA-ECMO is left ventricular distension — the LV is unable to eject against the increased afterload imposed by retrograde femoral arterial return, causing it to dilate, raising LVEDP and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure. This worsens pulmonary edema and can cause the aortic valve to stop opening entirely, creating a stagnant LV blood column at high risk for thrombus formation.</p><p>Concurrent LV unloading strategies are therefore often added to VA-ECMO in practice. The choice of unloading strategy depends on available resources, patient anatomy, and degree of LV distension.</p><ul><li><b>Impella alongside VA-ECMO</b> (ECMELLA): most common contemporary approach; Impella provides active transvalvular unloading while ECMO provides systemic support</li><li><b>Surgical LV vent</b>: inserted at the LV apex or pulmonary vein — used in central ECMO cannulation settings</li><li><b>IABP alongside VA-ECMO</b>: provides modest afterload reduction and counterpulsation; less effective than Impella for unloading</li><li><b>Monitoring for distension</b>: daily echo to confirm aortic valve opening, trending PCWP, chest X-ray for pulmonary edema progression</li></ul><p>Cohn is explicit that a stagnant, non-ejecting LV is not just a pump-mechanics nuisance but a clot factory &mdash; blood pools in a chamber that never empties, and intracardiac thrombus forms in a poorly contracting left ventricle or atrium precisely because little blood reaches the left side when right-atrial drainage is good. When that thrombus is discovered, it is removed at the time of durable-VAD implantation rather than chased on ECMO.</p><p>The venting options form a hierarchy by effectiveness. A simple <b>left-sided vent spliced into the venous limb</b> of the circuit can decompress the LA, but the book's observation is that <b>direct LV decompression is the most effective</b> form of left-sided drainage. In contemporary practice that means an <b>Impella across the valve (&quot;ECpella&quot;/ECMELLA)</b> for active transvalvular unloading; an <b>IABP</b> shaves afterload but unloads far less; a <b>percutaneous atrial septostomy</b> decompresses the LA; and a <b>surgical apical or pulmonary-vein vent</b> is used when the chest is open for central cannulation. Whichever is chosen, reducing ECMO flow to the minimum that still perfuses is part of the fix, since the circuit flow <em>is</em> the afterload.</p>"
    },
    {
      type: "checkpoint",
      q: "A patient on peripheral VA-ECMO develops worsening pulmonary edema and echo shows the aortic valve has stopped opening. What is the mechanism and recommended intervention?",
      options: [
        "ECMO oxygenator failure — replace the circuit",
        "LV distension; add active LV unloading",
        "Volume overload — increase diuresis",
        "Aortic stenosis progression — urgent TAVR"
      ],
      answer: 1,
      explain: "Non-opening of the aortic valve with worsening pulmonary edema on VA-ECMO indicates LV distension — the failing LV cannot generate enough pressure to open the aortic valve against the increased afterload from retrograde femoral arterial return. The LV is dilating, LVEDP is rising, and stagnant blood in the LV risks thrombosis. Adding LV unloading (Impella is the preferred contemporary approach) actively drains the LV and allows the aortic valve to open."
    }
  ]
};

window.LESSONS["d9-vad"] = {
  id: "d9-vad",
  day: 10,
  ord: 3,
  title: "Durable LVAD & Transplant",
  short: "LVAD",
  minutes: 17,
  chunks: [
    {
      title: "Durable LVAD: Design and Physiology",
      html: "<p>A durable LVAD is a permanent pump plumbed into the heart: it sucks blood from the LV apex and pipes it into the ascending aorta, doing the left ventricle's job for years in end-stage heart failure. The crucial design fact is that modern devices (the HeartMate 3) are <b>continuous-flow</b> — a single spinning rotor, like a fan or a water pump, not a squeezing bulb. It moves blood as a <b>steady river rather than a series of waves</b>.</p><p>That one engineering choice reshapes everything about the patient. Because the flow never pulses, <b>an LVAD patient may have no palpable pulse and no reliable pulse-ox reading</b> — blood pressure has to be taken with a Doppler probe, aiming for a MAP, not a systolic/diastolic. Nearly every unique LVAD complication traces back to this same non-pulsatile river: the smooth, high-shear flow that keeps the patient alive is also what shreds clotting proteins and coaxes fragile vessels to bleed, as the next chunks explain.</p><ul><li><b>Current standard</b>: HeartMate 3 — magnetically levitated centrifugal pump</li><li><b>HeartWare HVAD</b>: now discontinued for new implants</li><li><b>Non-pulsatile flow</b>: peripheral pulse pressure may be very narrow or absent — pulse oximetry may be unreliable; BP measured with Doppler probe targeting MAP greater than 70 mmHg</li><li><b>Device settings</b>: Pump speed (RPM) set to achieve cardiac output of 4-6 L/min while maintaining adequate aortic valve opening</li></ul><p>Cohn explains why continuous flow won out. Pulsatile pumps (like the HeartMate XVE) had many moving parts and multiple contact points, so <b>mechanical wear caused predictable failure between years 1 and 2</b> &mdash; few survived beyond two years. Continuous-flow devices are smaller, more efficient, and far more durable, and they proved the body tolerates non-pulsatility. Two families exist: an <b>axial-flow</b> design with an extracardiac pump housing (HeartMate II), and a <b>centrifugal</b>, magnetically levitated, discoid pump small enough for <b>intrapericardial</b> placement (HeartWare HVAD, and conceptually the HeartMate 3). The inflow cannula is cored into the <b>diaphragmatic LV apex &mdash; about one-third of the way from apex to base, lateral to the posterior descending artery</b> &mdash; and the outflow graft is sewn end-to-side to the ascending aorta with 5-0 polypropylene.</p><p>The single most exam-worthy physiologic fact: at constant speed, CF-pump output is exquisitely sensitive to the <b>pressure difference across the pump</b>, so <b>afterload governs flow</b>. Raise the patient's blood pressure and flow falls; this is why <b>MAP is titrated (Cohn targets ~60&ndash;80 mmHg)</b> with vasoactive drugs and volume rather than by chasing pump speed, and why <b>Doppler is the gold-standard non-invasive pressure measurement</b> &mdash; a cuff is unreliable and underestimates systolic pressure in a pulseless patient. The <b>pulsatility index</b> reflects residual LV contraction and <em>falls</em> as pump support increases. And the price of that smooth river is a new physiology: aortic smooth-muscle and elastic-fiber degeneration, and stasis with thrombus in the carotid bulb and aortic-valve cusps.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "MCS Strategy: BTR, BTT, and Destination Therapy",
      html: "<p>The indication for MCS — and therefore the intended duration of support — should be established at the time of device implantation. This strategy designation shapes all subsequent management decisions, including anticoagulation intensity, transplant listing priority, and the threshold for device exchange versus escalation to transplant.</p><p>The REMATCH trial (2001) established that permanent LVAD support meaningfully extends survival in patients with no other option, providing the foundational evidence for destination therapy. Subsequent device improvements have dramatically improved upon those early outcomes.</p><ul><li><b>Bridge to recovery (BTR)</b>: Short-term MCS for potentially reversible conditions (post-cardiotomy shock, acute myocarditis, ischemic stunning post-revascularization). Goal is ventricular unloading and end-organ support while awaiting myocardial recovery</li><li><b>Bridge to transplant (BTT)</b>: Durable MCS (typically LVAD) to maintain a patient on the transplant waiting list who cannot be sustained medically</li><li><b>Destination therapy (DT)</b>: Long-term LVAD in patients who are not transplant candidates — the REMATCH trial established this indication (48% reduction in all-cause mortality at 1 year vs. medical therapy alone)</li><li><b>MOMENTUM 3 trial</b>: HeartMate 3 vs. HeartMate II — HM3 had significantly lower pump thrombosis, stroke, and GI bleeding at 2 years, establishing HM3 as current standard</li></ul><p>Cohn puts REMATCH in context: completed in 2001, it compared the pulsatile HeartMate XVE against optimal medical therapy in transplant-ineligible end-stage HF and showed a <b>~48% reduction in mortality</b> &mdash; the first statistical proof of a survival benefit and the foundation of destination therapy. That opened the regulatory path: the continuous-flow HeartMate II earned FDA approval for <b>BTT in 2007 and for destination therapy in 2009</b>, and the HeartWare HVAD followed for BTT after the ADVANCE trial.</p><p>The strategy label also encodes <em>timing</em> and <em>patient selection</em>, not just intent. The book stresses that patients implanted while <b>decompensated (INTERMACS levels 1&ndash;2)</b> do markedly worse, so the goal is to optimize end-organs, nutrition, and infection &mdash; often with a temporary device or IABP first &mdash; before committing to a durable pump. Risk is quantified: the <b>HeartMate II Risk Score</b> (age, albumin, creatinine, INR, and center volume) stratifies 90-day survival at roughly <b>96% / 84% / 71%</b> across low/medium/high tiers, and INTERMACS registry survival on CF LVAD support runs about <b>80% at 1 year, 69% at 2 years, and 47% at 4 years</b> &mdash; the honest backdrop against which BTR, BTT, and DT decisions are made.</p>"
    },
    {
      type: "checkpoint",
      q: "The REMATCH trial established which LVAD strategy, and what was its primary finding?",
      options: [
        "Bridge to transplant — LVAD improved waitlist survival vs. inotropes",
        "Bridge to recovery — LVAD allowed myocardial recovery in ischemic cardiomyopathy",
        "Destination therapy; LVAD improved survival versus medical therapy",
        "Destination therapy — LVAD showed equivalent survival to cardiac transplantation"
      ],
      answer: 2,
      explain: "REMATCH (2001) was the first RCT to compare permanent LVAD support (HeartMate XVE, pulsatile device) versus optimal medical therapy in end-stage heart failure patients ineligible for transplantation. LVAD reduced all-cause mortality by 48% at 1 year, establishing destination therapy as a legitimate indication for durable MCS."
    },
    {
      title: "LVAD Complications: GI Bleeding and Stroke",
      html: "<p>Durable LVADs produce a unique pattern of complications driven by the physiology of chronic non-pulsatile flow. GI bleeding is the most common serious complication, arising from the combination of two mechanisms: non-pulsatile flow promotes formation of arteriovenous malformations (AVMs) in the GI tract, and continuous shear stress from the pump impeller destroys large von Willebrand factor multimers (acquired von Willebrand disease), impairing platelet-mediated hemostasis.</p><p>Stroke in LVAD patients can be embolic (from pump thrombus or device-related clot) or hemorrhagic (from anticoagulation combined with hypertension). The INR target for most devices is 2.0-3.0 on warfarin, creating a narrow therapeutic window between under-anticoagulation (pump thrombosis, embolic stroke) and over-anticoagulation (hemorrhagic stroke, GI bleeding).</p><ul><li><b>GI bleeding</b>: AVMs from non-pulsatile flow + acquired vWD; manage by reducing pump speed temporarily, correcting anticoagulation, GI endoscopy</li><li><b>Stroke</b>: Embolic or hemorrhagic; target INR 2.0-3.0; strict BP control (MAP 70-90 mmHg)</li><li><b>Initial management of GI bleed</b>: Hold anticoagulation (with care — pump thrombosis risk increases), reduce pump speed to allow some pulsatility, transfuse, GI consultation</li></ul><p>Cohn quantifies the GI problem: nonsurgical bleeding hits <b>18&ndash;30%</b> of CF-LVAD patients and <b>GI sources account for ~70%</b> of those episodes, split fairly evenly above and below the ligament of Treitz, with <b>AVMs and erosive gastritis</b> the leading lesions. Three mechanisms stack: obligatory anticoagulation; an <b>acquired von Willebrand syndrome</b> from proteolytic cleavage of high-molecular-weight vWF multimers (nearly universal with axial-flow pumps); and the loss of pulse pressure itself &mdash; a direct parallel to <b>Heyde syndrome</b>, where low pulsatility and mucosal hypoperfusion breed friable neovasculature, an association strongest in the first 3 months and tied to aortic-valve closure. Reassuringly, GI bleeding drives hospitalizations and transfusions but has <b>no proven mortality impact</b>. ISHLT guidance: hold anticoagulation during a significant bleed, resume after it resolves, and <b>reduce or stop it permanently for recurrent events</b>, dropping pump speed to restore some pulsatility while endoscopy hunts a frequently elusive AVM.</p><p>Stroke is the feared complication: incidence is about <b>11% at 1 year and 17% at 2 years</b>, with hemorrhagic and ischemic events roughly equal. Most presumed risk factors (anticoagulation intensity, AF, device type) have <em>not</em> held up statistically &mdash; the one consistently modifiable driver is <b>blood pressure</b>: patients discharged with lower BP and those on aggressive antihypertensives have significantly fewer neurologic events, which is why strict MAP control (targeting ~70&ndash;90 mmHg) is central. Embolic strokes may arise from thrombus in the stagnant aortic cusps or carotid bulb, dislodged when pulsatility returns &mdash; hence the value of intermittent washout. Stroke is not benign: it carries a <b>~25% 30-day and ~50% 1-year mortality</b>.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "Pump Thrombosis and Driveline Infection",
      html: "<p>Pump thrombosis is a potentially catastrophic complication in which clot forms within the pump mechanism, impeding flow and causing hemolysis. The HeartMate 3's magnetic levitation eliminates rotor-bearing contact and has dramatically reduced pump thrombosis rates compared to earlier devices, which was a central finding of the MOMENTUM 3 trial.</p><p>Driveline infection represents a unique vulnerability of external percutaneous LVADs. The driveline — the cable connecting the internal pump to the external controller — passes through the skin, creating a permanent portal for bacterial entry. Driveline infection rates increase with implant duration and can lead to bacteremia and pump endocarditis.</p><ul><li><b>Pump thrombosis signs</b>: Elevated LDH, plasma-free hemoglobin (hemolysis markers), low-flow alarms, hemodynamic deterioration, power spikes on device controller</li><li><b>Pump thrombosis treatment</b>: Intensify anticoagulation (heparin), consider thrombolytics, urgent pump exchange or transplant listing</li><li><b>Driveline infection</b>: Aggressive wound care protocol, targeted antibiotics, sometimes surgical debridement or driveline rerouting; bacteremia warrants pump pocket and bloodstream evaluation</li></ul><div class='kbox kbox-danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>DANGER</div><p>Pump thrombosis is a life-threatening emergency. Elevated LDH and plasma-free hemoglobin in an LVAD patient warrant urgent evaluation. Delay can lead to pump failure and hemodynamic collapse.</p></div><p>Cohn sharpens the diagnostic thresholds. INTERMACS separates <b>confirmed</b> thrombus (seen on inspection or imaging) from <b>suspected</b> thrombosis, defined by hemolysis, unexplained heart-failure symptoms, and abnormal pump parameters (power spikes). The hemolysis markers have cutoffs: <b>serum-free hemoglobin &gt; 40 mg/dL</b>, and <b>LDH &gt; 600 IU/L (about 2.5&times; the upper limit of normal)</b>, with LDH the more sensitive early signal. Contributing mechanisms are largely non-biologic &mdash; inadequate heat dissipation at low speeds, ineffective anticoagulation, and surgical/anatomic factors like <b>inlet angulation or outflow-graft kinking</b>. Treatment escalates from IV heparin or a direct thrombin inhibitor (<b>bivalirudin/argatroban</b>) through thrombolysis (which carries intracranial-bleed risk) to the definitive answer: <b>pump exchange</b> (subcostal or redo sternotomy), explant, or urgent transplant &mdash; and survival falls with each successive exchange.</p><p>Driveline infection is a game of time and outpatient discipline. INTERMACS reports percutaneous driveline infection in <b>~9.8%</b> overall, rising to <b>19% by 1 year and 1 in 4 by 2 years</b>, with a mean onset beyond 6 months &mdash; i.e., mostly at home once patients handle their own dressings. The single most protective surgical detail is <b>burying the velour-silicone interface of the driveline beneath the skin</b>, which anchors tissue ingrowth and lowers infection. Most cases are managed with targeted antibiotics, but <b>~12.5% need surgery</b> (vacuum-assisted closure, antibiotic beads, or driveline mobilization). Infection can raise 1-year mortality up to <b>six-fold</b>, yet a documented device infection <em>does not</em> preclude transplantation.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "Right Heart Failure After LVAD",
      html: "<p>Here is the cruel twist of LVAD surgery: fixing the left pump can sink the right one. Right heart failure hits 20–30% of patients early, and the mechanism is genuinely counterintuitive. Think of the two ventricles as pumps in series with a lake (the lungs) between them. While the LV was failing, it was a weak downstream pump — little blood got through the lungs, so the RV was actually handling a <em>light</em> load. Switch on the LVAD and you suddenly pull that downstream water through at full flow, and <b>all of it now floods back to the right ventricle</b> — which may not have the muscle to keep up with its new workload. The operation that rescued the left heart just overwhelmed the right.</p><p>Before LVAD, a failing LV created elevated pulmonary artery pressure and backed-up pulmonary venous hypertension — the RV was under increased pressure afterload but reduced preload (because the LV failure reduced forward flow through the lungs). After LVAD decompresses the LV, pulmonary artery pressure falls, but forward flow through the pulmonary circuit increases, delivering dramatically increased preload to a right ventricle that may not have the contractile reserve to handle it.</p><ul><li><b>Mechanism</b>: LV decompression restores forward flow through pulmonary circuit, increasing RV preload; simultaneously, leftward septal shift after LV unloading reduces RV geometry and septal contribution to RV contractility</li><li><b>Risk factors</b>: Pre-implant RV dysfunction, elevated PVR, severely dilated RV, low pre-op RVEF</li><li><b>Treatment</b>: Pulmonary vasodilators (milrinone, inhaled nitric oxide, sildenafil), RVAD (temporary right ventricular assist device), or BiVAD support</li></ul><p>Cohn quantifies the problem and formalizes the diagnosis. Post-LVAD RV failure occurs in <b>15&ndash;30%</b> of recipients, and <b>8&ndash;10% require mechanical RV support</b>. The HeartMate II trial definition is the need for RV support, inotropes for at least 14 days, or new inotropes started after day 14; the <b>Potapov criteria</b> are the inability to wean CPB, or any two of <b>MAP &lt; 55, CVP &gt; 16, SvO&sub2; &lt; 55%, LV flow index &lt; 2 L/min/m&sup2;, or inotrope dependence</b>. A telling bedside sign is <b>low LVAD flows despite adequate settings and normal volume</b> &mdash; the failing RV simply cannot fill the pump.</p><p>Because you cannot fix an RV mid-crash easily, the emphasis is on <b>predicting</b> it. Cohn lists preoperative red flags: ventilator support, elevated <b>CVP and CVP/PCWP ratio</b>, a reduced <b>RV stroke-work index</b>, high WBC and BUN, plus echo markers &mdash; reduced RV free-wall strain, high <b>RV-to-LV diameter ratio</b>, severe tricuspid regurgitation, and low RVEF (the Penn tool flags tachycardia, high CVP, ventilator, RV dysfunction, and TR). Management is preload reduction and RV protection: <b>inotropes (milrinone, dobutamine, isoproterenol, epinephrine)</b>, aggressive diuresis, strict maintenance of <b>sinus rhythm</b>, and inhaled nitric oxide to drop PVR. One counterintuitive pitfall &mdash; do <b>not</b> simply crank up LVAD speed, since faster unloading worsens the <b>leftward septal shift</b> that is stealing the RV's contractility.</p>"
    },
    {
      type: "checkpoint",
      q: "A patient develops right heart failure on post-operative day 2 after LVAD implantation. LVAD flows are low despite adequate pump settings. What is the most likely mechanism?",
      options: [
        "Pump thrombosis causing impaired LVAD output",
        "Aortic insufficiency causing LVAD recirculation",
        "Driveline infection causing septic cardiomyopathy",
        "RV preload stress plus leftward septal shift"
      ],
      answer: 3,
      explain: "After LVAD implantation, the restored forward flow through the pulmonary circuit dramatically increases RV preload, while leftward septal shift (after LV unloading) reduces the RV's geometry and the septal contribution to RV contractility. A pre-existing RV with limited reserve cannot accommodate this sudden increase. The result is RV failure and reduced LVAD preload — a vicious cycle where LVAD flows are low because the RV cannot fill the device."
    },
    {
      title: "Aortic Insufficiency and the LVAD Patient in Cardiac Arrest",
      html: "<p>Aortic insufficiency (AI) is a progressive complication of continuous-flow LVADs. The LVAD continuously unloads the LV and reduces LV systolic pressure — the aortic valve may never open or opens very infrequently. Chronic absence of valve opening leads to commissural fusion and progressive AI over months to years. Severe AI creates a recirculation loop where blood ejected by the LVAD into the aorta flows back through the incompetent aortic valve into the LV, is suctioned by the LVAD, and never reaches the systemic circulation effectively.</p><p>LVAD patients who appear to be in cardiac arrest present a unique emergency. Non-pulsatile flow means the absence of a palpable pulse does not indicate cardiac arrest — the pump may be running and providing adequate circulation. The assessment algorithm differs fundamentally from standard ACLS.</p><ul><li><b>Aortic insufficiency management</b>: Reduce pump speed (to allow some aortic valve opening), valve repair or replacement or closure procedure</li><li><b>Apparent arrest in LVAD patient</b>: Listen for pump hum first; use Doppler probe for carotid or radial artery flow; if pump running with Doppler flow, do NOT start chest compressions (risk of cannula displacement, pump damage)</li><li><b>If pump not running / no Doppler flow</b>: Standard CPR with care, call LVAD coordinator and cardiac surgery immediately</li></ul><div class='kbox kbox-danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>DANGER</div><p>Do NOT start chest compressions in an LVAD patient if the pump is running and Doppler flow is confirmed. Compressions can displace the inflow cannula, damage the pump, or cause hemorrhage at implant sites. Assess the pump first — always.</p></div><p>Cohn frames de-novo aortic insufficiency as a self-reinforcing failure. Because the pump keeps LV systolic pressure low, the aortic valve <b>opens rarely or never</b>, and a chronically shut valve undergoes <b>commissural fusion</b> &mdash; so the pump both suffers from AI (it degrades CF-pump performance) and <em>drives</em> it (it accelerates the valve's incompetence). The result is a closed recirculation loop: LVAD output ejected into the aorta washes straight back through the leaky valve into the LV, is re-suctioned, and never truly reaches the body. ISHLT recommends <b>concurrent intervention for moderate-or-greater AI</b> at implant &mdash; and, critically, a pre-existing <b>mechanical aortic prosthesis is prohibited</b> (thromboembolic risk in a rarely-opening valve) and must be surgically excluded, by oversewing the LVOT, a supravalvular patch, or a felt-sandwich plug; a bioprosthesis or central valve closure is the durable answer.</p><p>The arrest algorithm follows from the pulseless physiology. Absent pulse, unreadable oximetry, and an unreliable cuff mean the usual ACLS entry points fail &mdash; so the assessment reorders around the device: <b>listen for the pump hum, check controller power/battery and alarms, and use a Doppler for MAP</b>. If the pump is running with Doppler flow, the patient is being perfused and <b>chest compressions are contraindicated</b> (they risk dislodging the inflow cannula and causing catastrophic hemorrhage at the apex or outflow anastomosis). Only if there is <em>no</em> hum and no flow do you begin careful CPR while summoning the LVAD coordinator and surgeon. Note too that these patients often tolerate <b>ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation</b> for surprisingly long because the continuous-flow pump keeps perfusing &mdash; an awake patient in VT is a classic LVAD scenario.</p>"
    },
    {
      type: "checkpoint",
      q: "An LVAD patient is found unresponsive with no palpable pulse. The pump hum is audible. What is the correct immediate action?",
      options: [
        "Initiate chest compressions per standard ACLS",
        "Assess Doppler flow before deciding on CPR",
        "Defibrillate immediately",
        "Increase pump speed to maximum"
      ],
      answer: 1,
      explain: "LVAD patients have non-pulsatile flow — absence of palpable pulse does not indicate cardiac arrest. If the pump hum is audible, assess for Doppler flow in the carotid or radial artery. If Doppler flow is present, the device is providing circulation and chest compressions are contraindicated (risk of inflow cannula displacement, pump damage, hemorrhage). Identify the cause of unresponsiveness (arrhythmia, hypovolemia, RV failure, tamponade)."
    }
  ]
};

