window.LESSONS["d9-ecmo"].chunks.push(
  {
    level: 4,
    title: "Senior reasoning: match the device to the failure mode, and the VA-ECMO afterload paradox",
    html: "<p>The junior asks <i>which device is strongest</i>; the senior asks <i>which failure am I supporting</i> — because the wrong-physiology device makes a failing heart worse. Sort the problem into four buckets before you reach for a cannula.</p><ul><li><b>Isolated LV failure</b> → an active LV unloader (Impella CP/5.5) or IABP for a milder case; these <i>reduce</i> LV volume and MVO2.</li><li><b>Isolated RV failure</b> (post-LVAD, RV infarct, massive PE) → an RV-specific device (ProtekDuo/RVAD) or inotropic RV support with pulmonary vasodilators; do <b>not</b> answer RV failure with an LV device.</li><li><b>Biventricular or combined cardiorespiratory collapse</b> → VA-ECMO, which supports both circuits and adds an oxygenator.</li><li><b>Isolated respiratory failure with a good heart</b> → VV-ECMO; VA here needlessly loads the LV and risks Harlequin.</li></ul><p><b>The afterload paradox that defines VA-ECMO.</b> Peripheral (femoral) VA-ECMO returns blood <i>retrograde</i> up the aorta, so every liter of circuit flow is a liter of afterload the native LV must eject against. In a stunned LV this can push end-systolic pressure past what the ventricle can generate: the aortic valve stops opening, blood stagnates (thrombus risk), LVEDP and PCWP climb, and the lungs flood <i>while the systemic numbers look fine</i>. That is why the experienced surgeon watches the aortic-valve opening on echo and the PA pressures, not just the MAP — the ECMO that is saving the body may be drowning the heart, and the answer is a <b>venting strategy</b>, not more flow.</p><p><b>North-South (Harlequin) and the limb.</b> On femoral VA-ECMO with a recovering heart but injured lungs, native deoxygenated output supplies the coronaries and brain (upper body) while oxygenated circuit blood supplies the legs — check the <b>right radial</b> saturation, not a foot probe. And the arterial cannula strangles the leg it sits in: a <b>distal perfusion cannula</b> in the superficial femoral artery is mandatory, and a cold mottled foot means it is absent, kinked, or clotted.</p><div class='kbox kbox-study'><div class='kbox-tag'>STUDY</div><p>Weaning markers by device: for VA-ECMO, tolerating a flow-reduction trial with maintained pulsatility, an opening aortic valve, and adequate cardiac index; for the LV unloader, recovering native contractility on echo as speed is turned down. Recovery is proven by trial, not assumed by the calendar.</p></div>"
  }
);

/* ---- Acute pulmonary embolism (Ch21 Key Questions) — merged into Day-10 MCS/RV-failure ---- */
window.LESSONS["d9-ecmo"].chunks.push(
  {
    title: "Acute Pulmonary Embolism: Classification & Surgical Embolectomy",
    html: `<p>Acute PE is the sudden obstruction of the pulmonary arterial tree (usually thrombus from lower-limb/pelvic deep veins; occasionally air, fat, or amniotic fluid) — the <b>third commonest cause of cardiovascular death</b> after coronary disease and stroke. Prognosis tracks <b>haemodynamic compromise, not anatomic clot burden</b>, which is why the RV — not the CT clot volume — drives management.</p>
<p><b>Pathophysiology:</b> mechanical obstruction plus vasoactive mediators (thromboxane A₂, serotonin) raise RV afterload → RV wall-tension rises, the septum bows into the LV, LV filling and output fall → shock. Hypoxia arises from low output, V/Q mismatch, and right-to-left shunt through a PFO opened by high right-sided pressure.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>High-risk (massive), ~20%:</b> hypotension (SBP &lt;90 mmHg or a drop &gt;40 mmHg for &gt;15 min), cardiogenic shock, or collapse/arrest. 90-day mortality ~52%.</li>
  <li><b>Intermediate-risk (submassive), ~32%:</b> normotensive but with echocardiographic <b>RV dysfunction</b> or raised troponin.</li>
  <li><b>Low-risk (non-massive), ~48%:</b> normotensive, no RV strain, normal biomarkers.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Management:</b> start parenteral anticoagulation (LMWH/UFH/fondaparinux) immediately unless contraindicated. <b>Systemic thrombolysis</b> is first-line for high-risk PE (and for submassive PE that deteriorates) — but is <b>not</b> given for low-risk PE or stable submassive PE. Anticoagulate long-term (warfarin INR 2–3, or a DOAC); an IVC filter is for anticoagulation contraindication/failure or recurrent PE.</p>
<div class="kbox pearl">
  <div class="kbox-tag">SURGICAL EMBOLECTOMY — INDICATIONS & PRINCIPLES</div>
  <p>Indicated for <b>central</b> massive PE with a <b>contraindication to fibrinolysis</b>, or persistent shock/RV dysfunction <b>despite</b> fibrinolysis; also free-floating right-heart thrombus or impending paradoxical embolism through a PFO. Confirm a <b>centrally accessible</b> clot first — peripheral clot burden does not benefit from surgery. Median sternotomy, CPB with bicaval cannulation, often on a <b>beating heart without cross-clamp</b>; extract only <b>visible</b> thrombus (to the segmental level) via pulmonary-artery incisions — never blind-instrument the fragile PAs. Persistent RV failure afterwards may need an <b>RVAD or ECMO</b>.</p>
</div>
<div class="kbox">
  <div class="kbox-tag">ECG & ECHO CLUES</div>
  <p>ECG is normal in up to 30%; the classic <b>S1Q3T3</b> and V1–4 T-inversion (right-heart strain) appear in only a minority. Echo signs of RV dysfunction: RV dilation (RVEDD &gt;30 mm), <b>RV/LV ratio &gt;0.9</b>, septal flattening (D-shaped LV), and a TR jet &gt;2.6 m/s. D-dimer has high NPV (rules out) but low PPV — a positive D-dimer needs a CTPA.</p>
</div>`
  }
);

window.LESSONS["d9-vad"].chunks.push(
  {
    level: 5,
    title: "PGY-5 mastery: the escalation ladder and the distending LV on VA-ECMO",
    html: "<p>Escalate a failing heart <b>one rung at a time, each rung with its own trigger</b>, never jumping to the biggest device because the last one is failing:</p><ol><li><b>Optimize the reversibles</b> — preload, afterload, rhythm, AV synchrony, ischemia, tamponade. Trigger to climb: adequate filling and rhythm but cardiac index still &lt; ~2.0 with rising lactate.</li><li><b>Inotropes/pressors</b> (dobutamine or epinephrine for the LV, milrinone/iNO for the RV, norepinephrine for tone). Trigger: escalating doses (e.g., two agents at meaningful doses) with persistent hypoperfusion or malignant tachyarrhythmia.</li><li><b>IABP</b> for a modest ischemic gap; <b>temporary VAD / VA-ECMO</b> when the deficit is large or biventricular. Trigger: shock refractory to pharmacology, or you need an oxygenator.</li><li><b>Durable LVAD or transplant</b> once the heart declares non-recovery and end-organs are salvageable.</li></ol><p><b>The distending LV on peripheral VA-ECMO</b> is the exam favorite and the real 3 a.m. problem: rising PA pressures, a dilating non-ejecting LV, pink froth up the tube. Recognize that the systemic circuit is fine but the LV cannot open the aortic valve against retrograde flow. Reach for venting <b>in order of what is fastest and least invasive that will actually work</b>: (1) add an <b>Impella</b> across the valve for active transvalvular unloading (\"ECpella\") — the contemporary first reach; (2) an <b>IABP</b> to shave afterload if an Impella is not available (weaker); (3) a <b>percutaneous atrial septostomy</b> to decompress the left atrium; (4) a <b>surgical LV vent</b> (apex or pulmonary vein) when the chest is or will be open. Simultaneously reduce ECMO flow to the minimum that maintains perfusion, because flow <i>is</i> the afterload.</p><p><b>Post-LVAD RV failure</b> closes the loop: after you unload the LV, restored transpulmonary flow floods an RV of limited reserve while the septum bows left and steals its contractility. Support the RV (inotropes, iNO/pulmonary vasodilators, volume restraint) and <b>do not over-speed the LVAD</b> — a higher speed worsens the leftward septal shift and the RV. If it fails anyway, that is a BiVAD/RVAD, not a pump-setting problem.</p><div class='kbox kbox-danger'><div class='kbox-tag'>VIVA</div><p><i>\"Post-cardiotomy shock, on peripheral VA-ECMO, now the LV is not ejecting and the lungs are whiting out — what is happening and give me your venting options in order.\"</i> Answer: retrograde-flow afterload has arrested LV ejection → LV distension; vent by ECpella first, then IABP / atrial septostomy / surgical vent, and cut ECMO flow to the minimum that perfuses.</p></div>"
  }
);
